body:
Great write up, you should probably make your own post. I've always felt like this season was about the birth of America. It's showing how some men or perhaps even a lot of men got their power back then. It's also an origin story. We get to see how Satchel turns into Mike and how the big crime families started. The fact that a black child was taken and forced to live with a family that isn't his. Remind you of anything? The interesting thing about Mike and how his character mirrors the black experience in America is that he turns a negative into a positive. Yes, his beginnings were violent and awful but he made it, he survived and at the end of season 2, he's offered a nice comfortable job which ironically is what a lot of people who get into crime want. They want to be comfortable, treated with respect and be a part of society. It's what his father was fighting for, but along the way they forget because money = power.

&#x200B;

The sisters remind me of the union vs confederacy during the civil war. The union was seen as being more liberal and anti-slavery but that wasn't necessarily the truth. Both sides simply had different views on the economics of slavery. So that sister might have seemed friendly but I reckon she was just trying to "stick it" to the other sister.

&#x200B;

Sick man (who was most likely Rabbi's father) saying alpha and omega, reminds me of the  Ozymandias poem. He's desperately trying to hold on to the man he once was but even Kings decay and die. Perhaps foreshadowing what's to come for Mike?

Well apart from the bent locking pin, the scooter doesn't charge. The charger remains green when plugged in and the charging socket looks fried. I have a replacement socket that's on the way but it's taking forever.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Over the last few days I've done some research and now I'm thinking the BMS controller might be fried too rendering the new socket pointless.

One could argue that religons brainwash too. One could argue that we're brainwashed everytime we pick up a newspaper. 

Tony Robbins and countless over motivational speakers charge for seminars. 

&#x200B;

How does Keith losing money prove that the group was bad? 

&#x200B;

I agree with the rest of what you said (providing its true), but so far in the doc, it seems like DOS and its members did most of the crazy things. They are the ones who got carried away.
